<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Environment and Recycling</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> You Can Save Water By...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain abstract concepts, using simple analogies and native language when necessary. Concept: Interpret graphical data for class discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain the concept of recycling, using simple analogies, gestures, and brief phrases in the native language when necessary (e.g., showing circular hand motions to represent recycling as a "cycle" and saying "recycle" to reinforce understanding). Can interpret recycling data from visual aids, such as charts or graphs, by explaining key sections (e.g., pointing to a pie chart segment and saying, "This part shows how much plastic we recycle compared to paper.") Can use visual aids, such as flow charts, diagrams, and gestures to clarify abstract environmental concepts (e.g., using a flow chart to show the steps in the recycling process and gestures to emphasize the "cycle" of reuse).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <!-- STAGE 1: Warm-up / Pre-task -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing new concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing two images: one of a flowing river and another of a dry riverbed. Students discuss in pairs, "What happened?" "How can we protect rivers?" The teacher prompts for ideas on saving water. Students share actions they already know, such as "turn off the tap."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining the concept of "water waste" using a simple analogy: "Imagine a glass of water. If you drink some, you save some. If you spill it all, it's waste." The teacher uses gestures, e.g., showing a glass overflowing. Teacher models contrasting past and present actions: "In the past, people often wasted water. Today, we should be more careful." The teacher emphasizes stress contrasts between present ('waste') and past ('wasted').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I leave the water running while brushing my teeth, is that saving water or wasting water?" (Wasting water)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'wasted' happening now or before?" (Before)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 2: Presentation -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Environment and Recycling."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple infographic or flow chart titled "The Water Cycle and Human Impact" or "Recycling in Panama: What Happens to Our Waste?". The visual includes key vocabulary (waste, environment, clean, pollution, save) and simple numerical data (e.g., a pie chart showing typical household water usage or percentages of recycled materials). It also features images representing past vs. present practices and consequences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret graphical data for class discussions. Explain abstract concepts using visual aids.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (Whole class):</b> "What is the main idea of this infographic?" "What does it show about saving water?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Individual then Pair check):</b> Students answer True or False questions based on specific data points on the infographic (e.g., "True or False: Most water is used for showering." "True or False: In the past, people saved more water than today.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 3: Preparation / Practice -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Explaining concepts, interpreting data, giving advice, expressing cause-effect, contrasting past/present. Target Grammar: Past simple versus present practices, First conditional, Modal 'should'. Target Vocabulary: recycle, waste, environment, pollution, energy, landfill, planet, resource, reduce, reuse, clean, save, protect. Pronunciation: Stress contrasts (present/past), '-ed' endings (/d/, /ɪd/, /t/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences related to the infographic. They complete sentences using target grammar and vocabulary. Example: "In the past, people ____ (not / recycle) bottles. Today, people ____ (understand) the importance of it." "If we ____ (save) water, we ____ (protect) the planet." "You ____ (should / turn) off the lights to save ____ (energy)." The teacher monitors pronunciation of '-ed' endings like /d/ in 'saved', /ɪd/ in 'wanted', and /t/ in 'watched' when correcting.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Data Explanation Relay (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a simple chart showing "Water Use by Activity" (e.g., 40% showering, 30% toilet, 20% laundry, 10% other). Student A explains one section of the chart to Student B, using phrases like "This part shows..." and providing advice using "should." Student B listens and then explains the next section. They must use first conditional (e.g., "If we take shorter showers, we will save a lot of water.") and contrast past/present habits (e.g., "In the past, people didn't think about it, but now we should.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Concept Clarification (Group Work):</b> In small groups, students are given an abstract environmental concept (e.g., 'pollution', 'renewable energy', 'landfill'). They must brainstorm how to explain it to someone younger, using simple analogies, gestures, and drawing a quick diagram or flow chart. They then practice explaining it to each other, using brief phrases in native language (L1) if absolutely necessary for a difficult concept, and then the group gives advice about it using "should." Example: "Pollution is like too much trash in your house – it makes the environment 'sick'." (gestures to show 'sick').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 4: Performance / Production -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Environment and Recycling."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Presentation of "Environmental Action Plan" (21st Century Skills Project).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in their pre-assigned groups to present their Environmental Action Plan. Each group has prepared a visual aid (e.g., a poster, a digital presentation, a handmade flowchart) outlining their plan to promote sustainable living in their school or community, focusing on saving water and recycling. Each group member takes turns explaining a part of the plan.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must clearly explain their proposed actions (e.g., "We will reduce plastic waste by..."), using simple language and gestures to clarify abstract ideas (e.g., circular motion for 'recycle').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They need to interpret any data or statistics they have gathered or hypothesized (e.g., "Our chart shows that 70% of students didn't recycle in the past, but we predict it will increase to 90% if we implement this plan.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should use the first conditional to predict the impact of their actions (e.g., "If more people start recycling, we will reduce waste significantly and protect the environment.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must give advice using "should" (e.g., "Everyone should turn off the tap when brushing their teeth.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should contrast past practices with present awareness (e.g., "In the past, people didn't think much about saving energy, but today people are more aware.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Brief L1 intervention is permissible if a concept cannot be understood in English after several attempts, but students should be encouraged to exhaust English options first.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher facilitates by asking clarifying questions and encouraging other groups to listen carefully for specific details.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 5: Assessment / Post-task -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on individual and group mediation skills during the presentation. Peer assessment where groups provide constructive feedback to one another.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Group Presentation of the Environmental Action Plan.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Feedback):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity in explaining concepts (Mediation):</b> Can the student explain ideas clearly using simple language?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effective use of analogies and gestures:</b> Did they use non-verbal cues or simple comparisons to help others understand?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interpretation of visual aids/data:</b> Did they clearly explain what their charts/diagrams showed?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Appropriate use of target grammar:</b> Did they use past simple vs. present practices, first conditional, and 'should' correctly?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Did they incorporate words like 'recycle', 'waste', 'environment', 'save', 'protect'?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Was their speech clear, especially '-ed' endings and sentence stress?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Did the group work well together and contribute equally?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about explaining environmental actions (or data) today is that I need to..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can help my community save water by explaining that (e.g., 'if we save water, we protect our planet')..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 6: Reflection -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection (Oral Discussion):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did I explain my group's action plan to others today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about interpreting the data for my presentation, and how did I overcome it?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of mediation (explaining abstract concepts, interpreting data, using L1 briefly for clarification) were most successful for the students? What seemed most challenging?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the use of gestures and analogies effectively integrated into student explanations? How could this be enhanced in future lessons?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to systematically embed the target grammar (past vs. present, first conditional, should) and vocabulary into their explanations? What adjustments are needed for future mediation tasks?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "You Can Save Water By..." theme and the "Environmental Action Plan" project. The skills practiced here (explaining concepts, interpreting data, presenting solutions) will be crucial for future interdisciplinary projects or discussions on global issues that require students to mediate information and advocate for change.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>